summary: This documentation inside the GUI: open a page by name, click something to get its page, or press a key to learn what it runs.

The documentation is shipped with metafolder and read in the GUI, in the
[[help panel]]. Three commands lead to it or answer on the spot. They are part of
the shell, so they work before the help panel was ever shown.

!! Opening a page: `help:open`

<<key "help:open" "h h">> opens the help panel in the focused slot. With a text —
`help:open trash`, or `help trash` — it goes straight to a page when the text is the
''exact name'' of one, and searches every page for it otherwise:

* a page's title, such as `find in a panel` (case does not matter), or one of its other names: `ctrl-f`, `shortcuts`;
* a command, with or without arguments: `panel:open here treeref` opens the note of `panel:open`. A command with no note of its own leads to its panel;
* a panel type, when no page has that very name: `log-detail` opens the note of the `log-detail` panel.

A `#` in front always searches: `help #trash` lists the pages that mention the
trash.

!! Asking about something on screen: `help:cursor`

<<key "help:cursor" "h c">> — or <<key "help:cursor" F1>>, which also works while
typing — turns the pointer into a `?`. The next click does not reach what is
clicked; it opens the page about it: the page of the zone clicked when there is
one (the command input, the tab bar, the status bar, the finder or the query of
the list…), and the page of the panel otherwise. `Escape` gives up.

!! Asking about a key: `help:key`

<<key "help:key" "h k">> waits for a key and says what it runs, instead of running
it:

```
t l runs panel:open here metarecord-list — Set the focused slot's panel type
```

The answer is what the key would do ''now'': it is looked up for the panel that has
the focus, and for the text field being typed in, if any. A key that is bound, but
not here, says so:

```
f is not bound to any command here (elsewhere: file-manager:find in file-manager, trash:find in trash)
```

A sequence is followed to its end, its continuations listed above the status bar as
usual. `Escape` gives up — which is why `Escape` itself cannot be asked about. The
answer goes to the status bar, and so stays in the message log.

`help:cursor` and `help:key` each cancel the other: one question at a time.

!! The keys in the pages are yours

A page does not contain a key. It names the command, and the help panel writes the
keys bound to that command when it shows the page, read from your bindings as they
are at that moment ([[Keybindings]]). After a key is changed, the next page opened
says so; a command with no key reads ''unbound''. A page cannot go out of date with
`keybindings.toml`, the shipped one or yours.

!! Where the pages come from

They are this wiki: notes kept with the source code in `docs/wiki/`, rendered to
plain HTML and installed with the configuration in `~/.config/metafolder/docs/`
([[Shipped defaults in the source tree]]). Everything is installed, the developer
documentation included; the search leaves that part out unless asked.

